# Debug Root Cause

When debugging a failure, do not stop at a plausible hypothesis if direct evidence is available.

- If an error message says to check logs, Cloud Logging, Sentry, Datadog, CI output, browser console, or another external source, inspect that source before answering.
- Keep investigating until you can cite the exact failing command, exception, log line, status, or response that explains the failure.
- Final debugging answers must distinguish confirmed root cause from guesses and include the concrete evidence used.
- If the evidence source is unavailable or blocked, say exactly what blocked it and what command/tool should be run next.

## Failed-job debug trails

Every failed job on a telemetry-enabled cluster ships its full debug-event trail
(slot-ledger snapshots, trades, replacements, re-adds, stalls) to S3, one JSON
object per failure with keys `{project_id}/{YYYY-MM-DD}/{job_id}_{unix_ts}.json`:
`s3://burla-backend-debug-logs-018789813546` (prod, `--profile burla-prod`) or
`s3://burla-backend-debug-logs-002645521087` (test, `--profile burla-test`).
When asked to fix "the bug that just happened", start there:

```bash
aws s3api list-objects-v2 --bucket <bucket> --profile <profile> \
    --query 'sort_by(Contents, &LastModified)[-1].Key' --output text
aws s3 cp "s3://<bucket>/<key>" - --profile <profile> | python3 -m json.tool
```

A dev cluster's trail is also queryable directly in its worktree's
`_local_dev_state/history.db` (`debug_logs` table, one row per event).
